  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Thursday)\nIssued at 205 PM CDT FRI SEP 11 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are currently expanding across the\nregion, as multiple upper-level features keep the region\ndisturbed. Precipitable water values are also high, meaning that \nthese storms will remain very efficient rainfall producers. \nRainfall rates with some of the stronger afternoon thunderstorms \nwill be moderate to heavy at times, with most locations generally \nseeing less than an inch of rainfall. However, given the \naforementioned heavy rainfall rates, locally heavier amounts are \nexpected in a few isolated spots. Severe weather and flooding are \nnot expected through the weekend, and these showers and \nthunderstorms are nothing more than some much needed rainfall.\n\nUnfortunately, upper-level ridging will quickly begin to rebuild\nby Sunday, with rain chances dropping dramatically. While there\nwill still be a low chance (<30%) for an afternoon shower or\nthunderstorm, this activity will be the scattered, diurnally\ndriven activity we've seen for the last few weeks. Afternoon high\ntemperatures will quickly rebound as well, with highs climbing\ninto the low and mid-90s by Monday. This will essentially be the\ncopy/paste forecast for the rest of the week, as the apex of the\nridge will remain over the Tennessee River Valley into next\nweekend.\n\n/44/",
